WEBVTT

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:18.640
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.030
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.980
And takes me

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.950
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.070
To the home of wind

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.450
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.310
Little Jerry Buote from the
Creek was here this morning

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:08.400
and I told him I guessed
I'd hire him for the summer.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.870
Matthew, you said you would
adopt a boy to help out!

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.900
Goodbye, Bonny! Goodbye, Snow Queen!

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.650
Mr. Cuthbert, goodbye!

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.140
Goodbye!

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.200
I've made up my mind to enjoy this drive.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.130
If I make up my mind to, I definitely will.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:40.560
I am not going to think
about going back to the asylum

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.890
while we're having our drive.

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.930
Oh, look, there's one little early wild rose out!

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:54.020
Isn't it lovely? Don't you think
it must be glad to be a rose?

00:03:56.230 --> 00:03:58.530
Wouldn't it be nice if roses could talk?

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.040
I'm sure they could tell us such lovely things.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:07.470
And isn't pink the most
bewitching color in the world?

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:11.230
I love it, but I can't wear it.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.790
Redheaded people can't wear pink,
not even in imagination.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:22.070
Did you ever know of anybody whose
hair was red when she was young,

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:24.130
but got to be another color when she grew up?

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.700
No, I don't know as I ever did,

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:30.040
and I shouldn't think it likely to
happen in your case either.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.040
Well, that is another hope gone.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.520
My life is a perfect
graveyard of buried hopes.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.330
That's a sentence I read in a book once,

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:49.330
and I say it over to comfort myself
whenever I'm disappointed in anything.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.400
I don't see where the
comforting comes in myself.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:57.480
Why, because it sounds so nice and romantic...

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.850
Just as if I were a heroine in a book, you know.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.680
Are we going across the
Lake of Shining Waters today?

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.930
We're not going over Barry's pond,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.930
if that's what you mean by
your Lake of Shining Waters.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.160
We're going by the shore road.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.100
Shore road sounds nice!

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.260
Just when you said shore road I saw
it in a picture in my mind, as quick as that!

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:28.990
How far is it to White Sands?

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:30.730
It's five miles.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:35.870
And as you're evidently bent on talking
you might as well talk to some purpose

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:38.310
by telling me what you know about yourself.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.870
Oh, what I know about myself
isn't really worth telling.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.020
If you'll only let me tell you
what I imagine about myself,

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:47.310
you'll think it ever so much more interesting.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.540
No, I don't want any of your imaginings.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.620
Just you stick to bald facts.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:59.270
Begin at the beginning. Where were
you born and how old are you?

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:03.670
What's wrong?

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.690
Tell me!

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:07.470
No!

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.150
What's the point?

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:17.330
I don't want to think about those things.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:34.840
Is this the child from the orphanage?

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:40.000
But Rachel said that it was a boy.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.090
You hardly see Rachel like that,
mixing up a girl for a boy.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.250
No, it's not that.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:53.570
The true is... Something went wrong, and
she replaced the boy.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:57.730
Oh my! That is so unfortunate.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.080
But how did this happen?

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:02.300
I don't know myself!

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.950
That´s why I'm going to ask
Mrs. Spencer at White Sands

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.540
what this is about.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:13.970
Although it´s an error,
she has to be sent back.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.220
Poor child.

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:17.370
I'm sorry! I´m in a rush...

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:21.080
Anne! What´s wrong?

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.000
Anne! Anne! Come back!

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:26.370
Anne!

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.680
I´m sorry! I´ve wasted much of your time!

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.220
Well, I have things to do, so...

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:05.090
I´m sorry, Miss. Cuthbert.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.180
I´m all right now, please continue.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:15.500
I´m sorry too.
I never cared for your feelings.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.450
I´ll tell you about my past.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.120
I´ll talk from my birth.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:44.150
I was born in Bolingbroke, Nova Scotia.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.150
And I was eleven last March.

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:51.080
My father's name was Walter Shirley,

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.060
and he was a teacher in
the Bolingbroke High School.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:56.580
My mother's name was Bertha Shirley.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:00.590
Aren't Walter and Bertha lovely names?

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:05.920
I'm so glad my parents had nice names.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:09.660
It would be a real disgrace
to have a father named...well..

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:13.520
Say Jedediah, wouldn't it?

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:19.450
I guess it doesn't matter what a person's
name is as long as he behaves himself.

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:20.810
Well, I don't know.

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:29.110
I read in a book once that a rose by
any other name would smell as sweet,

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:31.410
but I've never been able to believe it.

00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:37.240
I don't believe a rose would be as nice if
it was called a thistle or a skunk cabbage.

00:10:38.110 --> 00:10:41.230
I suppose my father could have been a good
man even if he had been called Jedediah,

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:43.540
but I'm sure it would have been a cross.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:49.960
Well, that's it for the names.
Tell me about your parents.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:11:00.040
Well, my mother was a teacher
in the High school, too,

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:02.040
but when she married father

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.540
they went to live in a weeny-teeny
little yellow house in Bolingbroke.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:11.420
I've never seen that house,
but I've imagined it thousands of times.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:22.040
I think it must have had honeysuckle
over the parlor window,

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.980
and lilacs in the front yard,

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.400
and lilies of the valley just inside the gate.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.620
Yes, I didn't go there anytime, but
I can see it once I close my eyes.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:50.390
And muslin curtains in all the windows.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:55.080
Muslin curtains give a house such an air.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:02.070
I was born in that house.

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:07.990
Mrs. Thomas said I was the
homeliest baby she ever saw...

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:13.240
I was so scrawny and tiny and nothing but eyes.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:19.940
Although I still am now.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:25.990
But mother thought I
was perfectly beautiful.

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:28.610
I should think a mother
would be a better judge

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:32.210
than a poor woman who came
in to scrub, wouldn't you?

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:36.780
Any mother would love her own
child and think it beautiful.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:42.910
I'm glad she was satisfied with me anyhow...

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:49.270
I would feel so sad if I thought I
was a disappointment to her.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:53.440
Because she didn't live very
long after that, you see.

00:12:54.850 --> 00:12:58.820
She died of fever when I
was just three months old.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:06.220
I do wish she'd lived long enough for me
to remember calling her mother.

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:12.130
I think it would be so sweet to
say 'mother,' don't you?

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:25.140
I really wished I had someone
that I could call mother.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:27.480
Mother!

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:28.000
Or...

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:30.370
Mommy!

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:31.660
Or...

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:33.870
Mama!

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:35.510
Like that.

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:43.720
But I never even called my mother once.

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:47.010
It´s a bit too much for a 3 month old baby.

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:53.720
And father died four days
afterwards from fever too.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:59.180
That left me an orphan and
folks were at their wits end,

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:01.070
so Mrs. Thomas said, what to do with me.

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:13.630
You see, nobody wanted me even then.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:17.740
It seems to be my fate.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:33.980
Anne's words stopped here...

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:41.080
And Marilla didn't persue, while
wating patiently for Anne to speak.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:18.690
As Anne thinks of her mother and sighs...

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:22.410
She looks at the sea and
continues to talk about her past.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:28.670
Although she digresses at times.

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:36.900
But to Anne this was what was
needed to continue talking.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.100
Father and mother had both
come from places far away,

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:55.560
and it was well known they
hadn't any relatives living.

00:15:56.740 --> 00:16:02.970
Finally Mrs. Thomas said she'd take me,

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:07.460
though she was poor and
had a drunken husband.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:20.200
Mr. and Mrs. Thomas moved away
from Bolingbroke to Marysville.

00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:26.790
I helped look after the Thomas
children younger than me.

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:33.660
And I can tell you they
took a lot of looking after.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:39.910
Because Mrs. Thomas was
busy working everyday.

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.540
And Mr. Thomas didn't know how
to take care of children.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:03.780
Then Mr. Thomas was killed
falling under a train.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:14.730
Mrs. Thomas was at her wits end, so
she said, what to do with me.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.660
Then Mrs. Hammond from up the river came,

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:29.300
and said she'd take me, seeing
I was handy with children,

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:35.670
and I went up the river to live with her
in a little clearing among the stumps.

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:39.930
It was a very lonesome place.

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:43.470
I'm sure I could never have lived there
if I hadn't had an imagination.

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:53.260
Mr. Hammond worked a little sawmill up there,

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:57.200
and Mrs. Hammond had eight children.

00:17:57.730 --> 00:18:01.370
She had twins three times.

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:10.800
I like babies in moderation, but twins
three times in succession is too much.

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:16.700
I told Mrs. Hammond so firmly,
when the last pair came.

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.020
I used to get so dreadfully
tired carrying them about.

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:23.920
I lived up river with
Mrs. Hammond over two years,

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:28.410
and then Mr. Hammond died and
Mrs. Hammond broke up housekeeping.

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:35.950
She divided her children among her
relatives and went to the States.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:55.860
I had to go to the asylum at Hopetown,
because nobody would take me.

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:08.740
They didn't want me at the asylum, either.
they said they were over-crowded as it was.

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:16.100
I was there four months until Mrs. Spencer came.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:28.990
Did you ever go to school?

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:30.130
Yes.

00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:37.260
But not a great deal.

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:43.250
When I went up river we were so far from
a school that I couldn't walk it in winter

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:49.540
and there was a vacation in summer, so I
could only go in the spring and fall.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:55.810
But I can read pretty well and I know ever
so many pieces of poetry off by heart.

00:19:55.810 --> 00:20:01.820
I loved the books my parents gave me,

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:05.600
they made me understand many things.

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:09.180
But I accidentally mistook them for rubbish

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:12.330
and threw them away when I went to the asylum.

00:20:18.890 --> 00:20:22.180
Well...were those women...

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:26.980
Mrs. Thomas and Mrs. Hammond... good to you?

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:32.200
Oh, they meant to be...

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:35.820
I know they meant to be just
as good and kind as possible.

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:40.800
And when people mean to be good to you,

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:45.660
you don't mind very much
when they're not quite always.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:49.950
They had a good deal to worry them.

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:54.460
It's very trying to have a
drunken husband, you see.

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:59.570
And it must be very trying to have twins
three times in succession, don't you think?

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:05.410
But I feel sure they
meant to be good to me.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:15.880
Marilla asked no more questions.

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:21.960
Anne gave herself up to a silent
rapture over the shore road,

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:28.840
and Marilla guided the sorrel abstractedly
while she pondered deeply.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:14.450
Isn't the sea wonderful?

00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:20.360
Once, Mr. Thomas hired an express wagon

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:23.990
and took us all to spend the day
at the shore ten miles away.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:30.170
I enjoyed every moment of that day, even if I
had to look after the children all the time.

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:35.160
I lived it over in happy dreams for years.

00:22:57.100 --> 00:22:58.790
Aren't those gulls splendid?

00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:01.240
Would you like to be a gull?

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:03.190
I think I would.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:07.690
Don't you think it would be
nice to wake up at sunrise,

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:13.090
and swoop down over the water and
away out over that lovely blue all day.

00:23:14.060 --> 00:23:17.360
And then at night to fly back to one's nest?

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:24.620
What big house is that just ahead, please?

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:27.200
That's the White Sands Hotel.

00:23:27.530 --> 00:23:32.250
There are heaps of Americans
come there for the summer.

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.520
I was afraid it might be Mrs. Spencer's place.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:39.360
I don't want to get there.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:43.330
Somehow, it will seem like
the end of everything.

00:23:52.340 --> 00:23:55.660
But as matters went...

00:23:56.250 --> 00:24:02.240
the carriage continued proceeding to
Mrs. Spencer's place at White Sands Cove.

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:21.080
Mrs. Spencer discovers it
was entirely a misunderstand,

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:24.970
and an old lady who wanted
to adopt Anne appeared.

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:29.490
Next of Anne of Green Gables:
Marilla Makes Up Her Mind.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:30.480
Please watch!
